course: dictionary
lesson: 11013
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A word with a capital letter, like Waterloo, is a name. In «News of the Battle of Waterloo in 1815 took days to reach London», the words Battle of tell you that Waterloo is a place where a battle was fought. It is a town in Belgium.
explain: Context clues help you choose the right meaning. Waterloo can mean a town, a railway station, a song or a defeat. Read the words around it: a station means a building for trains, and a song is something you can sing or listen to.
explain: Look at «meet your Waterloo». The small letter and the words meet your tell you it is not a place. It means a final defeat after a long run of success. Swap in your meaning: "The champions met their final defeat in the last round." It still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: Sentences like «News of Lincoln's death in 1865 took about 12 days to reach Europe» show how news travelled slowly in the past. Then «after the Atlantic telegraph cable of 1866 news crossed in minutes». Context shows that the first event is the one that took days.
fact: Waterloo is a town in Belgium where Napoleon was finally defeated in 1815.
fact: Waterloo Station is a big railway station in London.
fact: «Meet your Waterloo» means to suffer a final defeat after a long run of success.
fact: "Waterloo" is also a famous 1974 song by the Swedish band ABBA.
exercise: «News of the Battle of Waterloo in 1815 took days to reach London.» What is Waterloo here? | A railway station in London | A famous 1974 song | *A town in Belgium where a famous battle was fought | A final defeat after a long run of success
exercise: Sam says, "The team had won every game, but they met their Waterloo in the final." What does meet their Waterloo mean? | To win the first game of the year | *To suffer a final defeat after a long run of success | To sing a song with a band | To catch a train at a big station
exercise: "Mia caught the train from Waterloo to visit her gran." What is Waterloo here? | *A big railway station in London | A song by ABBA | A famous battle in 1815 | A town in Belgium
test: Why does Waterloo start with a capital letter in «the Battle of Waterloo»? | Because it is a very long word | Because it is a name of a place | Because it starts a sentence | *Because it is a name of a place and the battle is named after it
test: Which sentence uses waterloo as a noun meaning a final defeat? | We caught the train at Waterloo | Waterloo is a town in Belgium | ABBA sang "Waterloo" on the radio | *The champions met their waterloo in the last round
exam: According to «News of the Battle of Waterloo in 1815 took days to reach London», how long did the news take to reach London? | Seconds | Minutes | Years | *Days
